now labour have won back the red bull  seat of wakefield from the conservatives  in last night's by-election with a  majority of almost five thousand nhs  worker simon lightwood will now  represent the west yorkshire city and  the commons labour leader sakir stama  has said that the vote shows the country  in his words has lost confidence in the  tories well we can speak now to the  shadow leveling up secretary lisa nandy  lisa mandy good afternoon to you a red  brick back in the wall for wakefield  i think what's really significant for us  about this result is we obviously had a  great candidate and we ran a great  campaign  and there was a lot of distaste for the  prime minister that was reflected to us  on the doorsteps but  this is our best result in wakefield  since 2001 it's our best ever result on  current boundaries more than a 12 swing  which if replicated across the country  will put labour on course to form a  majority government of course we're not  complacent we'll go out and fight for  every vote as we did in wakefield come  the next election but this is a big  boost to us because  we didn't just see people turning away  from the tories we also felt very much a  mood on the ground i felt it yesterday  when i was knocking on doors that people  were looking to labor again and in many  instances they were saying we really  like what we see we think this is a  party that will back us and deliver for  us in government in a way that the  tories just haven't done  well 12 swing you say it is it is  significant but that was the same swing  we saw uh when labour won it back under  corbyn  well we we've had a storming result  there's just no way around it we were  quietly confident we weren't complacent  you should have seen the effort  yesterday there were just teams out  trying to get people to um to come out  and vote but crucially what we were  finding was that people people often had  already gone and done it they they liked  simon they liked his message but most of  all i think they just felt increasingly  that the country can't go on with a  government that doesn't back it or  support it most people now are dealing  with the cost of living crisis most  families and businesses struggling to  make ends meet and they'd heard things  like the campaign that we led for the  windfall tax they'd heard us even on  issues like the bus and rail strikes  where the tories have very much tried to  to blame labour for it and they'd heard  us saying get round the table and sort  this out and back people who want to get  to work and who run our railways and  people were responding to that message  so if i were the tourists i would be  worried about this result not just  because of boris johnson's deep on  popularity but because i think there is  genuinely a feeling that this is a  government that just simply isn't  working yeah but given all the you know  the trail of uh  what many of the electric view to be  disasters under boris johnson's  government um the tories lost twice as  many votes as labour gained in that  by-election it could have gone better  well i mean of course every election can  go better but it's hard to see how we  could have imagined a better result than  this especially three years ago when we  had this very painful break  with people who'd voted labour all their  lives through successive generations and  felt that they couldn't do again when  that happened and we lost seats like  wakefield and knew that it was painful  but i also wondered if it was permanent  what we saw this this time was not just  a lot of people saying we can't vote for  the tories but we did see people coming  back to us and i was hearing that on the  doors everywhere that i went people  did have a real sense that labour is  rooting for them and that we've got  ideas that will help to solve the very  real problems that they've got i think  for me this is very much a sign that  people are looking not just for an  opposition to this government but for an  alternative and where we've been putting  forward alternatives such as getting  around the table and sorting out the  rail strikes such as the windfall tax  and getting money back into people's  pockets and trying to create growth in  the economy that's what people have  responded to and so you can expect to  see a lot more from us on that over the  coming months so what is the labour  party strategy uh in the run-up to the  next  general election now we can see the  potency of tactical voting there might  we see some electoral pacts  well we we didn't have an electoral pact  in this election and nor would we people  vote with their feet they decide what  they want to do and i think you know  people wanting to get rid of boris  johnson was clearly a big driver in both  of these by-elections but there was more  than that as well there were people  coming back to labor they weren't just  looking at us again they were coming  back to us and having vowed not to do so  three years ago  and that is that is a big moment for the  labour party and for the labour movement  they've heard that we've dealt with some  of the issues that were rightly costing  us votes at the last general election i  think there is a feeling that we've you  know we've we've worked very hard to try  and repair that relationship and that  we're going to deliver for them when we  say that we're going to invest 28  billion a year every year over a decade  to get good jobs back into places like  wakefield and other parts of the north  and the midlands i think people really  do respond to that they believe that  we're rooting for them and that we're  coming up with the plans and the ideas  to support them you know wakefield  yesterday was a really you know it's  difficult there's a bus strike on  there's a rail strike on  people really struggling to get out but  they came out in order to show that they  think things can be different and we're  determined to be the party that gives  voice to that so  may i ask that may i ask um is  kierstarma the man to win a general  election the most recent yougov poll uh  finds that 51 percent of adults say he  is doing badly as labour leader and very  tellingly i think 19 don't know  well you know he's became leader in the  middle of a pandemic and we've been  we've been playing catch-up ever since  but i think this is really a significant  moment for us because  people were very much responding to  labour not just voting against the  conservatives and of course we'd never  be complacent about that but the joke  with boris johnson really is starting to  wear thin things that people thought  were funny about him the fact that he  makes these big bold claims and doesn't  really deliver that he mucks around when  um you know makes jokes and wise cracks  and likes the political knockabout i  think people have had enough of that  actually they're looking for serious  politicians for serious times and i  think there is very much a sense that  kierstama is a serious credible  politician who will do what he says he  will do and that you can trust him at  his word  you mentioned you mentioned the strikes  that are paralyzing the country at the  moment very pertinently um he's got a  bit of a problem with leadership there  hasn't he because he's told that his  party uh none of you are going to go and  join the picket line and yet some of his  own front benchers did so  well look i mean i don't think he's got  a problem with leadership at all because  he's been out as has louise hague our  shadow transport secretary and many  others making the case that rail workers  have been doing their jobs they have  been trying to keep our railways going  through the pandemic they've been  fighting for better safety and security  on the railways and a fair deal  for living standards the public are  trying to do their jobs they're trying  to get to work in the face of these  crippling devastating strikes and what  we're doing as a labour party is our job  which is exerting maximum pressure on  the government in parliament and  elsewhere and in wakefield yesterday to  say get round the table and sort this  out because you're the only people who  can that's us doing our job that's the  public doing their job now we need the  government to start doing their job
 

